the second lesson is quite short  and introduces the most common ways of  greeting people kamasava and kamatalivu  one is formal one is not so formal  if we look at kamasava  the first thing to realize is that sa is  just  a contraction of  is simply how  is it going  which is normally used with people that  we already know quite well  notice that it's coma we don't pronounce  the t  however as we saw in the previous lesson  when we say come on vu  we're really asking how  are you going  and here we do pronounce the t because  it's followed by a vowel  now we get our first look at the past  tense in french  and the phrase in the lesson said atu  weekend  so we see that it's like have you spent  a good weekend it's very similar the  word order is very similar to english  have you  passed or spent a good weekend and the  answer is  so uh with all these verbs that we've  highlighted here in orange  you will have to learn how they change  and the different forms  and fortunately more and more  dictionaries  on the web  offer conjugations or you can google for  conjugations of these different verbs  that should be enough to get you started  the most important thing is to listen  over and over again to the lesson to  save a lot of words and phrases to your  database for review in your flashcards
 

